BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collar-works-elizabeth-murray-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collar Works: Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency (EMAR) 20
 27
DESCRIPTION:The Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency gives emerging\, underrep
 resented and established artists an immersive\, communal atmosphere for ar
 t-making and dialogue on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, New York\, the clas
 sic farmhouse where the late Elizabeth Murray spent more than two decades 
 of summers and kept her studio in the large\, cathedral-like dairy barn. M
 urray\, who died in 2007\, won a Skowhegan Medal in Painting in 1986\, a M
 acArthur grant in 1999 and a MoMA career retrospective in 2005\; her husba
 nd Bob Holman is a poet and arts activist who founded the Bowery Poetry Cl
 ub. In 2017 the Murray-Holman family partnered with Collar Works\, a nonpr
 ofit contemporary arts organisation in Troy\, New York\, to build a summer
  residency carrying the legacy forward. EMAR is explicitly designed around
  the critical needs of emerging and underrepresented artists and of artist
 s with children\, with an active commitment to diversity\, equity\, access
 ibility and inclusion\, and describes itself as a safe space for creative 
 risk-taking\, personal growth and respite. Work is primarily in visual and
  literary arts\, though the programme supports new contemporary work acros
 s many art forms. TWO TRACKS: the INDIVIDUAL RESIDENCY takes up to five ar
 tists per session (including onsite artist-staff) for two weeks\, with 24-
 hour access to semi-private studios in the barn\, a private bedroom in the
  farmhouse\, shared bathrooms\, communal living room\, kitchen and a libra
 ry holding Elizabeth Murray's personal art book collection. The FAMILY RES
 IDENCY invites three to four artists and their families for one week\, wit
 h a private bedroom (families may need to room together)\, a semi-private 
 studio in the same barn\, and full-day childcare running as a parallel cre
 ative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, focused on art and nature prog
 ramming: while parent artists get uninterrupted studio time\, children wor
 k in their own EMAR Kids Art Studio\, explore outside through guided activ
 ities\, play\, swim\, read and take guided field trips to local farms and 
 museums. Partners are welcome to join and help with childcare\, but the pr
 ogramme is committed to fully supporting artists who want or need to atten
 d solo with their children\, and Collar Works works case by case with each
  artist to put the necessary support and equipment in place before arrival
  given the rural setting. APPLICATION requires artist information\, a stat
 ement of intent (250 to 500 words)\, an artist biography (250 words)\, ten
  examples of recent work (visual arts as JPEGs up to 2MB each\, with a PDF
  of links or a curated URL page for video and media works including any pa
 sswords\; literary arts as up to three poems totalling max six pages\, or 
 max ten pages of prose) and a list of works with title\, medium\, date and
  size. Applications go through Submittable and close 1 October 2026 (the S
 ubmittable listing shows a 2 October 2026 06:00 close in its own timezone)
 . Questions and technical assistance: info@collarworks.org.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Collar Works\nLocation: In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Wash
 ington County\, New York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray
  and Bob Holman). Collar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.\nAward:
  Fully supported: there is no cost to participate for accepted artists. Pr
 ivate bedroom accommodation with shared bathrooms in the farmhouse\, 24-ho
 ur access to a semi-private studio in the barn that housed Elizabeth Murra
 y's studio\, all food provided (dinner cooked and eaten communally)\, and 
 use of the property including a swimming pool\, pond and 77 acres of field
 s and woods. Accepted artists also receive a USD 500 stipend to use at the
 ir discretion towards travel\, materials or other associated costs. The Fa
 mily Residency additionally provides free full-day (9am to 3pm) childcare 
 as a parallel creative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, plus a dedica
 ted Kids Art Studio. APPLICATION FEE: USD 35 (the first month of the appli
 cation period was free\; the fee applies from 1 August 2026).\nDuration: T
 wo weeks for individual artists\, one week for families. 2027 individual s
 essions: Session 1\, 15 to 29 May 2027\; Session 2\, 1 to 15 June 2027. 20
 27 family session: Session 3\, 19 to 26 June 2027. Finalist interviews Nov
 ember 2026\; final notification December 2026.\n\nMore: https://collarwork
 s.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-artist-residency-2027?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collarworks.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-arti
 st-residency-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Washington County\, New
  York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray and Bob Holman). C
 ollar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:al-purdy-aframe-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: Al Purdy A-frame Association: Al and Eurithe Purdy Reside
 ncy 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Al Purdy A-frame Residency offers Canadian writers time and
  space to work at the historic A-frame home built by poet Al Purdy and his
  wife Eurithe on Roblin Lake in Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Onta
 rio. Poetry is prioritized\, but all writers are welcome. SUPPORT: CAD 750
  per week stipend and paid return travel (subject to the Association's fun
 draising and Canada Council matching)\; residencies run 3 to 12 weeks. ELI
 GIBILITY: Canadian citizens or permanent residents only. DEADLINE: 18 Sept
 ember 2026 (postmark\, or 4:00pm PT for electronic submissions). Apply via
  the residency page.\n\nOrganisation: The Al Purdy A-frame Association\nLo
 cation: Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Ontario\, Canada. Canadian c
 itizens or permanent residents only.\nAward: CAD 750 per week stipend plus
  paid return travel to Ameliasburgh (funding subject to the Association's 
 fundraising and Canada Council matching). Terms of 3 to 12 weeks. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: 3 to 12 weeks\n\nMore: https://www.alpurdy.ca/resid
 ency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.alpurdy.ca/residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Ontario\, Canada. Canadian c
 itizens or permanent residents only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ditrapano-finch-manuscript-lab-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260711
SUMMARY:Deadline: Giancarlo DiTrapano Foundation: The Finch Manuscript Lab 
 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Finch Manuscript Lab is a 2.5-month residency supporting wr
 iters completing book-length manuscripts\, hosted at the Finch Country Hou
 se in Serramezzana\, Italy\, and designed as the final sprint in the marat
 hon of writing a book. It offers living and work space\, writing time in a
  tranquil setting\, creative community\, and professional development incl
 uding manuscript consultations\, craft discussions\, and publishing semina
 rs. Residency dates: 1 October to 15 December 2026\; residents will be sel
 ected by 30 July 2026. FUNDING: thanks to the support of the Goethe Instit
 ute and Culture Moves Europe\, all selected residents receive a stipend of
  EUR 2\,280 for the residency period (EUR 30 per day in residence\, paid w
 eekly)\, a travel allowance of EUR 400 for distances under 5\,000 km from 
 Serramezzana or EUR 800 for distances over 5\,000 km\, an additional EUR 4
 00 for green travel (e.g. taking trains instead of planes)\, and additiona
 l support of EUR 200 for parents of children under 18. ELIGIBILITY: applic
 ants must be over 18 and a legal resident of one of the 39 Culture Moves E
 urope eligible countries\, excluding Italy\; this includes all EU member s
 tates other than Italy and 13 participating non-EU countries (Albania\, An
 dorra\, Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina\, Georgia\, Iceland\, Kosovo\, Mo
 ldova\, Montenegro\, North Macedonia\, Norway\, Serbia\, and Tunisia). APP
 LICATION (via Submittable\, in order of importance): a short sample of pas
 t work (any length\, though only the first 30 pages may be read)\; a brief
  note\, up to one page\, explaining the manuscript or book project to be w
 orked on in residency\, where you are in the creation process\, and why yo
 u are interested\; an excerpt of the book project not exceeding 30 pages (
 prose\, poetry\, mixed media\, photography\, or any other form of artist's
  book is acceptable)\; and a CV in English. NOTE ON DEADLINE: the foundati
 on's text gives the open call as 15 June to 10 July 2026\, while the Submi
 ttable page lists a close of Wednesday 15 July 2026 at 12:00 PM\; apply by
  10 July to be safe.\n\nOrganisation: The Giancarlo DiTrapano Foundation f
 or Literature and the Arts\nLocation: Finch Country House\, Serramezzana\,
  Italy\; open to legal residents of the 39 Culture Moves Europe eligible c
 ountries\, excluding Italy\nAward: EUR 2\,280 stipend for the residency pe
 riod (EUR 30 per day in residence\, paid weekly)\, plus travel allowance o
 f EUR 400 (under 5\,000 km) or EUR 800 (over 5\,000 km)\, an additional EU
 R 400 green-travel top-up (e.g. trains instead of planes)\, and EUR 200 ad
 ditional support for parents of children under 18. Supported by the Goethe
  Institute and Culture Moves Europe.\nDuration: 2.5 months\, 1 October to 
 15 December 2026\n\nMore: https://ditrapanofoundation.submittable.com/subm
 it/171cc54d-b582-47a3-93a4-47c46b65904b/the-finch-manuscript-lab?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ditrapanofoundation.submittable.com/submit/171cc54d-b582-47a3-9
 3a4-47c46b65904b/the-finch-manuscript-lab?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Finch Country House\, Serramezzana\, Italy\; open to legal residen
 ts of the 39 Culture Moves Europe eligible countries\, excluding Italy
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:upstream-creative-writers-grassroots-journalism-residency-2026@artifici
 alnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
SUMMARY:Deadline: Upstream Creative Writers / Grassroots Journalism Residen
 cy 2026: Writing About Natural Places\, Stewards\, and the Spaces We Care 
 For
DESCRIPTION:The Upstream Creative Writers / Grassroots Journalism Residency
  invites creative writers\, poets\, storytellers and grassroots journalist
 s to spend intentional time over nine months exploring Minnesota's parks\,
  waterways\, forests and natural places\, and the people who care for them
 . The residency seeks writers who care about storytelling rooted in place\
 , environment\, community\, stewardship and connection. SELECTED MINNESOTA
  ARTISTS RECEIVE: a USD 3\,000 artist stipend\; mileage reimbursement up t
 o USD 500\; a Minnesota State Parks Pass\; mentorship and creative support
 \; and the opportunity to publish and share their work statewide. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: applicants must be a Minnesota resident\, 18 years or older\, with a 
 residence in East Side St Paul\; West Central Minnesota (Becker\, Clay\, D
 ouglas\, Grant\, Otter Tail\, Pope\, Stevens\, Traverse\, and Wilkin Count
 ies)\; or the Iron Range\, Minnesota (Aitkin\, Carlton\, Cook\, Itasca\, K
 oochiching\, Lake\, and Saint Louis Counties). Applications open 8 June 20
 26\; deadline 6 July 2026 by 11:59pm. Apply via the online JotForm.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Upstream\nLocation: Remote residency in Minnesota\, USA\; appli
 cants must be Minnesota residents living in East Side St Paul\; West Centr
 al Minnesota (Becker\, Clay\, Douglas\, Grant\, Otter Tail\, Pope\, Steven
 s\, Traverse\, and Wilkin Counties)\; or the Iron Range (Aitkin\, Carlton\
 , Cook\, Itasca\, Koochiching\, Lake\, and Saint Louis Counties).\nAward: 
 USD 3\,000 artist stipend\, plus mileage reimbursement up to USD 500\, a M
 innesota State Parks Pass\, mentorship and creative support\, and the oppo
 rtunity to publish and share work statewide. No application fee.\nDuration
 : 9-month remote residency\n\nMore: https://form.jotform.com/2614629664421
 60?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://form.jotform.com/261462966442160?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote residency in Minnesota\, USA\; applicants must be Minnesota
  residents living in East Side St Paul\; West Central Minnesota (Becker\, 
 Clay\, Douglas\, Grant\, Otter Tail\, Pope\, Stevens\, Traverse\, and Wilk
 in Counties)\; or the Iron Range (Aitkin\, Carlton\, Cook\, Itasca\, Kooch
 iching\, Lake\, and Saint Louis Counties).
END:VEVENT
